Covalence bridge atomically precise metal nanocluster and metal-organic frameworks for enhanced photostability and photocatalysis
Aimin Yao, Yuanxin Du (✉), Meng Han, Yan Wang, Jiashen Hu, Qingtao Zhu, Hongting Sheng, and Manzhou Zhu (✉)
Department of Materials Science and Engineering, Centre for Atomic Engineering of Advanced Materials, Key Laboratory of Structure and Functional Regulation of Hybrid Materials of Ministry of Education, Key Laboratory of Functional Inorganic Material Chemistry of Anhui Province, Anhui University, Hefei 230601, China
© Tsinghua University Press 2022  Received: 7 May 2022 / Revised: 15 June 2022 / Accepted: 1 July 2022
## ABSTRACT
Metal nanoclusters (NCs) with precise structure and ultrasmall size have attracted great interests in catalysis. However, the poor stability has limited its large-scale use. Herein, we proposed the "covalence bridge" strategy to effectively connect atomically precise metal NCs and metal-organic frameworks. Benefiting from the covalent linkage, the synthesized UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) showed outstanding stability after 16 h photocatalysis. Moreover, the covalence bridge created a strong metal-support interaction between the two components and provided an effective charge transport channel and thereby enhanced photocatalytic activity. UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) displayed an exceptional photocatalytic H\(_2\) production rate, which is 21 and 90 times higher than that of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/Au\(_{25}\)(PET)\(_{18}\) (made by physically combination) and bare UiO-66-NH\(_2\), respectively. Thermodynamic and kinetic studies demonstrated that UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) exhibited higher charge transfer efficiency, lower overpotential of water reduction and activation energy barrier compared with its counterparts.
## KEYWORDS
atomically precise nanoclusters, metal-organic frameworks, covalent binding, photostability, photocatalytic hydrogen production
## 1 Introduction
Atomically precise metal nanoclusters (NCs) as a prototype with unique size-dependent optical and electronic properties and well-defined composition and structure have attracted extensive attention, especially in catalysis. In comparison to conventional metal NCs/nanoparticles, atomically precise metal NCs not only can be efficient catalysts with high catalytic activity, but also act as model catalysts to reveal structure-activity relationship [1–3]. The ultrasmall size of NCs endows high surface-to volume ratio and abundant surface atoms, contributing to its high catalytic activity. However, the ultrasmall size is a double-edged sword which induces the high surface free energy and poor stability of NCs, leading to the aggregation and performance loss during the catalytic process [4–6]. Therefore, how to pursue high activity as well as keep good stability at the same time is still a puzzle in NCs-based catalysis.Introducing porous matrix to support NCs has been considered as a potential method to improve its stability and catalytic activity. Compared with traditional porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with ultrahigh surface area, highly ordered structure, and tunable chemical functionalities hold great promise to immobilize and uniformly separate NCs [7–9] One of the common methods to encapsulate NCs into MOFs is in situ chemical reduction metal precursor in the pre-synthesized MOFs [10, 11]. By this method, the NCs are usually located on the outer surface of the MOF and easy to fall off, which hinders recycling use [12]. Besides, this method can hardly guarantee the atomic accuracy of NCs, because elaborate thermodynamic selection, kinetic-control reduction, and size-focusing growth or separation procedure are generally required to synthesize precise NCs [7]. The other mainstream method is epitaxial growth MOFs on the pre-synthesized NCs via coordination interaction or electrostatic attraction [8, 12]. Due to the poor thermal stability of NCs, this method only can be applicable to the MOFs that can be synthesized at low temperature, resulting in a huge limitation in the type of composites. Therefore, it is essential to develop an effective strategy to integrate NCs and MOFs.Owing to the diversity of functional groups in NCs and MOFs, it provides an opportunity to build a bridge to combine NCs and MOFs based on the covalent bonding. This bonding form can not only strongly stabilize NCs, avoid aggregation and detachment in catalysis, but also initiate strong metal–support interaction to create synergies and boost the catalytic activity. In this work, we have proposed the "covalence bridge" strategy to improve the stability and activity. Previously, Jiang et al. utilized Au–carbene covalent bonds to in situ synthesize Au NC/UiO-68-NHC (NHC = N-heterocyclic carbene). However, the cluster obtained by in situ preparation hardly possesses precise structure [5]. Therefore, we pre-synthesized atomically precise Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) NCs (L-Cys is short for L-Cysteine) and bonded with UiO-66-NH\(_2\) via amide covalence link. Due to the strong linkage, the UiO-
Introducing porous matrix to support NCs has been considered as a potential method to improve its stability and catalytic activity. Compared with traditional porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with ultrahigh surface area, highly ordered structure, and tunable chemical functionalities hold great promise to immobilize and uniformly separate NCs [7–9] One of the common methods to encapsulate NCs into MOFs is in situ chemical reduction metal precursor in the pre-synthesized MOFs [10, 11]. By this method, the NCs are usually located on the outer
surface of the MOF and easy to fall off, which hinders recycling use [12]. Besides, this method can hardly guarantee the atomic accuracy of NCs, because elaborate thermodynamic selection, kinetic-control reduction, and size-focusing growth or separation procedure are generally required to synthesize precise NCs [7]. The other mainstream method is epitaxial growth MOFs on the pre-synthesized NCs via coordination interaction or electrostatic attraction [8, 12]. Due to the poor thermal stability of NCs, this method only can be applicable to the MOFs that can be synthesized at low temperature, resulting in a huge limitation in the type of composites. Therefore, it is essential to develop an effective strategy to integrate NCs and MOFs.
Owing to the diversity of functional groups in NCs and MOFs, it provides an opportunity to build a bridge to combine NCs and MOFs based on the covalent bonding. This bonding form can not only strongly stabilize NCs, avoid aggregation and detachment in catalysis, but also initiate strong metal–support interaction to create synergies and boost the catalytic activity. In this work, we have proposed the "covalence bridge" strategy to improve the stability and activity. Previously, Jiang et al. utilized Au–carbene covalent bonds to in situ synthesize Au NC/UiO-68-NHC (NHC = N-heterocyclic carbene). However, the cluster obtained by in situ preparation hardly possesses precise structure [5]. Therefore, we pre-synthesized atomically precise Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) NCs (L-Cys is short for L-Cysteine) and bonded with UiO-66-NH\(_2\) via amide covalence link. Due to the strong linkage, the UiO-
66-NH\(_2\)-Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) exhibited excellent stability after long-term photocatalytic hydrogen evolution compared with its counterpart made by physically mix (UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/Au\(_{25}\)(PET)\(_{18}\) (PET is short for 2-phenylethanethiol). In addition, the "covalence bridge" played a role of charge transport channel, which facilitates photogenerated e-h\(^+\) separation and suppresses the recombination of them. Therefore, benefiting from the strong metal–support interaction, the UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) showed extraordinary H\(_2\) production rates (17012.045 \(\upmu\)mol·g\(^{-1}\)·h\(^{-1}\)), which is 21 times higher than that of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/Au\(_{25}\)(PET)\(_{18}\).
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\)
UiO-66-NH\(_2\) and Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) were synthesized according to previous reports [13, 14]. UiO-66-NH\(_2\)-Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) was prepared by dehydration condensation reaction. Au\(_{25}\)(L-Cys)\(_{18}\) solution (1 mg, 1 mL H\(_2\)O) was added into the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) solution (10 mg, 10 mL H\(_2\)O), then 0.5 g 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl) carbodiimide (EDCl) and 0.177 g N-hydroxybenzotriazole (HOBt) were added, and the mixture was rapidly stirred at room temperature for 2 h. Finally, the product was collected by centrifugation, washed with ethanol and dried in a vacuum oven at 50\(^{\circ}\)C.
## 2.2 Synthesis of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/Au\(_{25}\)(PET)\(_{18}\)
\(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) was synthesized according to our previous report [15]. UiO-66-NH\(_2\)/Au\(_{25}\)(PET)\(_{18}\) was synthesized by chemical impregnation method, Au\(_{25}\)(PET)\(_{18}\) solution (1 mg, 1 mL dichloromethane (DCM)) was added into the UiO-66-NH\(_2\) solution (10 mg, 10 mL DCM) and stirred vigorously at room temperature for 2 h. Finally, the product was collected by centrifugation and dried in a vacuum oven at 50\(^{\circ}\)C.
## 2.3 Characterization
Ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) absorption spectra were recorded on a Techcomp UV1000 spectrophotometer. Morphology of sample was observed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2100). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were performed by an ESCALAB 250Xi XPS system. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were obtained with Cu Kα radiation on SmartLab 9KW. Loading amount was determined by inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS, iCAP 7400 Duo). Electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra were obtained from JEOL JES-FA200 ESR spectrometer. Fluorescence spectra at different temperatures were obtained from Hitachi F-7000 fluorescence spectrophotometer with low temperature accessory. The femtosecond pump-probe transient absorption (TA) measurements were performed using a regenerative amplified Ti:sapphire laser system (coherent; 800 nm, 70 fs, 6 mJ·pulse\(^{-1}\), and 1 kHz repetition rate) as the laser source and a femto-TA100 spectrometer (Time-Tech Spectra).
