## 2.4 Photoelectrochemical measurements
Photocurrent, Mott–Schottky, and Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests were tested on CHI760D (Shanghai, Chen hua Co., Ltd., China) electrochemical workstation. 2 mg catalyst was dispersed in 2 mL ethanol containing 10 \(\upmu\)L nafion, and the mixed solution (100 \(\upmu\)L) was uniformly coated on indium tin oxide (ITO) glass as working electrode for photocurrent test, or (10 \(\upmu\)L) dropped on the glassy carbon electrode for Mott–Schottky and EIS tests. Using the three-electrode test method, the counter electrode was Pt plate; the reference electrode was Ag/AgCl; the electrolyte was 0.1 M Na\(_2\)SO\(_4\). The light source was 300 W Xenon lamp with a cut-off filter (> 420 nm). The photocurrent was measured at bias potential of +0.5 V and under chopped light with 10 s intervals. The test frequency of Mott–Schottky was 500, 1,000, and 1,500 Hz, and the test of EIS was under dark conditions, and the voltage was -1.5 V.
## 2.5 Photocatalytic hydrogen production
5 mg catalyst, triethanolamine (TEOA) (100 mL, 10 wt.%) as sacrificial reagent, and 0.02 g Er B as photosensitizer were mixed uniformly, and the suspension was stirred and purged with nitrogen for -20 min to remove air. The light source was a 300 W Xenon lamp with a > 420 nm filter. For hydrogen generation, 200 \(\upmu\)L of the headspace was injected into the gas chromatography (S Kexiao GC-1690, using high-purity Ar as the carrier gas) using a thermal conductivity detector (TCD) and was quantified by standard curve.
## 3 Results and discussion
The "covalence bridge" strategy was carried out to link atomically precise metal NCs and MOFs and construct strong metal–support synergetic interaction to achieve the stabilization of NCs as well as the improvement of catalytic performance (Scheme 1). The Au\(_{25}\)(L-
The formation of amide covalent bond in \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) was further verified by Fourier transforms infrared (FT-IR) and XPS. For \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\), it showed several characteristic peaks, which were located at 3,350 and \(3,465\;\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ascribed to the symmetric and asymmetric \(\mathrm{N-H}\) stretching modes [16] at 1,255 and \(1,332\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) corresponded to the \(\mathrm{C-N}\) stretching [17], and at
\(1,050\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) attributed to the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) rocking vibrations, respectively [18]. For \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\), a new peak at \(1,678\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) emerged, indicating the formation of amide \(\left(-\mathrm{NH-C=O}\right)\) (Fig. 2(a)) [19]. In addition, the N 1s XPS spectrum of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) displayed a new peak at \(401.5\;\mathrm{eV}\), also confirming the formation of amide bond (Fig. 2(b)) [19]. However, \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) did not exhibit the typical amide feature peak in FT-IR XPS spectra (Fig. S3 in the ESM). Furthermore, an obvious charge transfer took place between \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\,\mathrm{NCs}\) and \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\). For \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\), the binding energy of \(\mathrm{Zr}\,3\mathrm{d}\) showed \(\sim0.30\;\mathrm{eV}\) decrease compared with \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\), indicating the increase in electron density of \(\mathrm{Zr}\). In comparison to the \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\,\mathrm{NCs}\), Au 4f and S 2p peaks of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) showed about 0.32 and 0.31 eV positively shift, suggesting the reduction in electron density of Au and S (Figs. 2(c)–2(e)). The XPS peak shifts are attributed to the interface electron transfer between \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) and \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\), confirming the strong metal–support synergistic interaction due to the existence of covalence bridge, which is beneficial for the transfer of photogenerated electron–hole pairs, so as to improve the photocatalytic activity [20]. Nevertheless, all of the chemical environment of \(\mathrm{Zr}\), Au, and S in \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) were almost unchanged after \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\,\mathrm{NCs}\) combining with \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\), which is because that there is only simple physical contact between them (Fig. S4 in the ESM).
From the Mott–Schottky plots, the conduction band (CB) of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\), and \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) were estimated to be \(-0.73\), \(-1.81\), and \(-0.90\;\mathrm{V}\) (vs. NHE, \(\mathrm{pH}=6.8\)), respectively, which are all lower than the potential of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) reduction \((-0.40\;\mathrm{V}\) vs. NHE, \(\mathrm{pH}=6.8\)), indicating that they are suitable for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production (Fig. S5 in the ESM) [14, 21]. Therefore, the photocatalytic performance of as
prepared samples was evaluated by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production from water splitting. Under visible light irradiation, pristine UiO-66-NH2 exhibited negligible \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production rate \((\sim\,188.425\,\,\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) . After covalence binding with \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) NCs, the photocatalytic activity was remarkably improved. The \(0.25\,\mathrm{\wt.\%}\) \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) NCs loading on the UiO-66-NH2 showed the maximum \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production rate of \(\sim\,17,012.045\,\mathrm{\,\,\upmumol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}}\) , 90 times higher than bare UiO-66-NH2, and 21 times as high as physical combination of similar content \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) NCs and UiO-66-NH2 (Fig. 3(a), and Figs. S6–S8 in the ESM). Besides, UiO\(66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}\) performed higher \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation rate than other previous reported catalysts (Table S1 in the ESM). The results indicate that Au NCs can effectively enhance the photocatalytic activity, and the covalent linkage is much more favor for charge transfer, suppressing e–h recombination and thus for better photocatalytic performance. Moreover, UiO-66-NH2- \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) retained well-satisfied \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production rate in the consecutive four runs, while the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production rate of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) was greatly decreased by \(70\%\) only after one cycle (Fig. 3(b)). The loss in catalytic activity of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) is mainly due to the aggregation and shedding of clusters caused by weak physical binding during photocatalysis (Fig. S9 and Table S2 in the ESM). After photocatalytic reaction, UiO-66-NH2-Au25(L-Cys)18 maintained the structure, morphology, and chemical environment as before (Figs. S9 and S10 in the ESM). However, the chemical state of Au and \(Z\mathbf{r}\) in UiO-66-NH2/Au25(PET)18 exhibited significant change. The Au 4f XPS peaks showed \(\sim\,0.26\,\mathrm{\eV}\) positively shift, while Zr 3d XPS peaks showed \(\sim\,0.28\,\mathrm{\eV}\) negatively shift, compared with the original UiO-66-NH2/Au25(PET)18 (Fig. S10 in the ESM). These results reveal that UiO-66-NH2-Au25(L-Cys)18 exhibits enhanced photostability due to the covalence bridge.
To explore the mechanism of the enhanced photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production activity of UiO-66-NH2-Au25(L-Cys)18, we investigated it from the aspects of thermodynamics, the generation, separation, and transfer efficiency of e–h pairs. It is well known that introducing cocatalyst can reduce \(E_{\mathrm{R}}\) (the overpotential for water reduction) and thus improve the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production rate on the basis of thermodynamic principle. The on-set overpotential of hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is the reflection of the ability of a cocatalyst to lower \(E_{\mathrm{R}}\) [14, 22]. As can be seen from the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves, the UiO-66-NH2-Au25(L-Cys)18
exhibited lowest on-set overpotential, indicating that UiO-66-NH2- \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) was thermodynamically superior to UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) and UiO-66-NH2 (Fig. 3(c)). Besides, after combining with Au NCs, the absorption spectrum of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) basically remained the original profile and showed the characteristic peak of Au NCs, and the absorption edge of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) displayed a significant red shift, and UiO-66-NH2-Au25(L\(\mathrm{Cys})_{18}\) had the maximum light harvesting (Fig. S11(a) in the ESM). The corresponding Tauc plot reveals that the Eg of UiO-66-NH2- \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) is \(2.47\ \ \mathrm{eV}\) , which is lower than UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) \((2.56\ \mathrm{\eV})\) and UiO-66-NH2 \((2.89\ \mathrm{\eV})\) (Figs. S11(b)–S11(d) in the ESM). In addition, UiO-66-NH2-Au25(L\(\mathrm{Cys})_{18}\) possessed the strongest photocurrent response followed by UiO-66-NH2/Au25(PET)18 and UiO-66-NH2, indicating that UiO\(66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{Cys})_{18}\) can promote the generation of more photoexcited charges (Fig. 3(d)). The electron transfer kinetics also need to be taken into account. The smallest interfacial charge transfer resistance of UiO-66-NH2-Au25(L-Cys)18 confirmed its best electron transport capability (Fig. 3(e)). The steady-state photoluminescence (PL) intensity followed the order: UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\,>\,\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\,>\,\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18},\) also demonstrating the rapidest charge separation in UiO-66-NH2- \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) (Fig. 3(f)). From both thermodynamics and kinetics aspects, all of these results demonstrate that benefiting from the covalence bridge, UiO-66-NH2-Au25(L-Cys)18 exhibited stronger ability of carriers generation, more effective e–h separation and higher charge immigration efficiency.
ESR, ultrafast TA, and temperature-dependent PL spectroscopy were further used to insight into the mechanism. Under visible light irradiation, all samples showed a new ESR signal at \(g\,=\) 2.0023, corresponding to the generation of oxygen-centered active sites in Zr-oxo clusters through link-to-cluster electron transfer (LCCT). [14, 23] The ESR signals of UiO-66-NH2 and UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) were similar and relatively weak, while UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) exhibited a stronger signal, which is attributed to the acceleration of photoinduced electron transfer (Fig. 4(a)). Ultrafast TA spectroscopy was conducted to track in real time the charge transfer dynamics. For all samples, the TA spectra profiles were similar with no essential variations under different probe delays. When probed at the same delay times, UiO-66-NH2- \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) exhibited a weaker stimulated emission (a negative signal in the range of 450 and \(550~\mathrm{nm}\) ) compared with UiO-66-
\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) and \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\), suggesting the efficient quenching excited state by covalent linked \(\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{L-Cys})_{18}\) NCs (Fig. 4(b) and Fig. S12 in the BSM). This is in the accordance with the PL emission spectra, which also demonstrates that covalent linked Au NCs have the stronger ability to quench the PL of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) than physically connected Au NCs, as shown in Fig. 3(f). From the TA kinetics traces of excited state absorption (ESA) at
\(650\ \ \mathrm{nm}\), \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}{-}A u_{25}(L{-}C y s)_{18}}\) exhibited the shortest average relaxation lifetime of 56.6 ps, indicating that the fastest charge transfer between \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) and Au NCs, again consistent with its highest photocatalytic activity (Fig. 4(c)). UiO- \(66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) showed higher activity than pure UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), however the excited state charge carrier dynamics of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Au}_{25}(\mathrm{PET})_{18}\) was not superior to pure MOF. Therefore, its
high photocatalytic activity is mainly attributed to its strong light absorption and low overpotential for water reduction (Fig. 3(c) and Fig. S11 in the ESM).
In addition, the activation energy \((E_{\mathrm{a}})\) was used to evaluate the difficulty of a reaction combining both thermodynamic and dynamic factors. With the help of temperature-dependent PL and Arrhenius equation, the \(E_{\mathrm{a}}\) of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}-Au_{25}(L-Cys)_{18}}\), \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}/Au_{25}(PET)_{18}}\), and \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) were estimated as 34.95, 35.60, and \(43.76\,\mathrm{meV}\), respectively (Fig. 4(d) and Figs. S13 and S14 in the ESM). The result demonstrates that covalent linked Au NCs can effectively reduce the reaction energy barrier and improve the activity.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, a "covalence bridge" strategy was developed to effectively connect atomically precise NCs and MOF. Take \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}-Au_{25}(L-Cys)_{18}}\) as the example, with the help of the amide covalent linkage, it not only provides the assurance for the long-term photostability, but also offers strong metal–support interaction for enhanced photocatalytic activity. A series of photoelectrochemical and spectroscopic experiments were conducted to reveal the underlying reason of the performance difference between \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}-Au_{25}(L-Cys)_{18}}\) and its counterparts from both thermodynamic and kinetic aspects. Due to the formation of covalence bridge, \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}-Au_{25}(L-Cys)_{18}}\) can effectively lower the overpotential for water reduction, reduce the reaction energy barrier, promote charge separation, suppress e–h recombination, and thus higher photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production activity. This work not only provides inspirations for developing NCs-MOFs with high activity and stability, but also offers in depth understanding on the effect of link way between NCs and MOFs on thermodynamic and charge transfer kinetics process.
